# ALLOWLIST, NOT A BLOCKLIST.
#
# This repo is public. Everything is ignored by default and only the files named below can ever
# be committed, so a stray file dropped into this folder cannot reach the open web by accident.
# Add a new line here deliberately or it does not ship.
*

!.gitignore
!.nojekyll
!README.md
!*.html
!robots.txt
!sitemap.xml
!CNAME

# The social preview card, generated by build-site.ps1 from the live headline numbers.
# Named explicitly rather than allowing *.png: the allowlist only works while it stays tight.
# Without this line the og:image URL 404s and every shared link renders as bare grey text -
# which is what happened on the first publish of the social tags.
!og.png

# Partner and Playbooki logos, fetched from each brand's own site. Named as a folder because
# the set grows whenever a referral is added, and a per-file line would be forgotten.
#
# *** THIS LINE WAS MISSING FOR THREE CONSECUTIVE PUSHES ON 6 Nov 2026 AND NOBODY SAW IT. ***
# The logos were committed-looking - git add -A reported nothing unusual, the local build
# embedded the right <img> paths, and the pages rendered correctly on disk - but the allowlist
# above silently dropped every file in brand/, so all four 404'd on the live site and the
# onerror fallback quietly swapped each one back to plain text. The fallback is what hid it:
# the site never looked broken, it just never looked different.
#
# TWO REAL LESSONS. The first is that a file:// preview cannot load these assets either, so
# "it renders locally" proved nothing about deployment and the only honest check is fetching
# the URL off the live host. The second is that a graceful degradation path makes a missing
# file invisible - which is the right behaviour for a reader and the worst one for whoever is
# trying to tell whether the deploy worked.
!brand/
!brand/*

# The Artifact-only copy of the home page. It has no <!doctype>, <html>, <head> or <body> -
# the Artifact publisher supplies those - so served as a real web page it renders in quirks
# mode with no charset and no viewport. index.html is the real home page; this is not.
artifact-index.html
